              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing production flowchart**\n\nI'm considering how a production flowchart for industrial potato chip manufacturing works. It involves processes like slicing, deep frying, and inspection before washing. I’m mapping out these steps: washing and peeling before slicing to remove surface starch, dewatering after rinsing, seasoning post-frying, and cooling before packaging. I want to make sure the flowchart is legible with unique anchors for each node. I'll check to ensure each anchor relates to its position clearly, and there’s no redundancy in anchors.**Mapping production flowchart**\n\nI’m outlining a production flowchart for making bagged chips, connecting process nodes with arrows. First, raw potatoes undergo sorting and inspection to handle defects. Next, they get washed — peeling can be optional for some skin-on chips. Slicing releases surface starch, which I need to rinse off before frying to avoid issues. After frying, I apply seasoning while the chips are still warm. Finally, I have to cool the chips before packaging to prevent condensation. I should check the order of these steps to ensure everything flows well.**Refining the seasoning process**\n\nI’m thinking through the seasoning process for potato chips. Typically, seasoning happens after frying and draining excess oil, while the chips are still warm. I may need a visual chain that includes both seasoning and cooling. My steps are sorting, washing before slicing, rinsing off starch, dewatering, frying, draining oil, seasoning, and then cooling before packing. However, I want to ensure the seasoning anchor stands out, as it seems the original flavor refers specifically to adding salt. I need to clarify this in my process.",
